## Phase 4: Event System Hardening (Weeks 7-10)

- [x] 4.1 Audit all Redis pub/sub event handlers
  - [x] Search `arbiter/` and `elitea_core/events/` for all event subscriptions
  - [x] List every `event_node.subscribe()` / `@event_node.on()` handler
  - [x] For each handler, document: event name, source, consumer count, idempotency requirements
  - [x] Output audit as a table in AGENT.md under "Event Audit"
  - [x] Identify handlers that would break with duplicate delivery (non-idempotent)

- [x] 4.2 Classify events: broadcast vs work vs notification
  - [x] From audit in 4.1, tag each event as one of:
    - `broadcast` — all pods must process (e.g., cache invalidation)
    - `work` — exactly one pod must process (e.g., task execution)
    - `notification` — best-effort, loss acceptable (e.g., UI updates)
  - [x] Add classification column to audit table in AGENT.md
  - [x] Identify which `work` events are currently broadcast (bug candidates)
  - [x] Create `elitea_core/events/event_classification.py` with enum and registry

- [x] 4.3 Implement Redis Streams for work events
  - [x] Create `elitea_core/utils/redis_streams.py`
  - [x] Implement `StreamProducer.publish(stream_name, event_data)` using XADD
  - [x] Implement `StreamConsumer.consume(stream_name, group, consumer_name)` using XREADGROUP
  - [x] Implement `StreamConsumer.ack(stream_name, group, message_id)` using XACK
  - [x] Add MAXLEN trim on publish (configurable, default 10000)
  - [x] Handle consumer group creation with XGROUP CREATE (MKSTREAM)
  - [x] Write unit tests (>=85% coverage) — 61 tests, 98% coverage

- [x] 4.4 Migrate task distribution to Streams
  - [x] Find current task distribution mechanism (grep `distribute_task`, `task_queue`, event handlers)
  - [x] Replace pub/sub broadcast with Stream-based work queue
  - [x] Stream name: `work:task_distribution`
  - [x] Consumer group: `task_workers`
  - [x] Each pod claims tasks via XREADGROUP (exactly-once delivery)
  - [x] ACK after successful processing, NACK on failure
  - [x] Write unit tests (>=85% coverage) — 49 tests, 93% coverage

- [x] 4.5 Add event deduplication (SETNX pattern)
  - [x] Create `elitea_core/utils/event_dedup.py`
  - [x] Implement `is_duplicate(event_id, ttl_seconds=300)` using SETNX
  - [x] Key pattern: `event_dedup:{event_id}` with TTL
  - [x] Return True if already processed, False if new
  - [x] Add decorator `@deduplicate(ttl=300)` for event handlers
  - [x] Write unit tests (>=85% coverage) — 60 tests, 100% coverage

- [x] 4.6 Implement idempotency keys
  - [x] Create `elitea_core/utils/idempotency.py`
  - [x] Implement `IdempotencyStore.check_and_set(key, result, ttl=3600)`
  - [x] If key exists: return cached result (skip processing)
  - [x] If key absent: set key, process, store result
  - [x] Key pattern: `idempotency:{operation}:{hash_of_params}`
  - [x] Add `@idempotent(key_func, ttl)` decorator for API handlers
  - [x] Write unit tests (>=85% coverage) — 60 tests, 100% coverage

- [x] 4.7 Implement dead letter queue
  - [x] Create `elitea_core/utils/dead_letter_queue.py`
  - [x] On Stream consumer failure after N retries (default 3): move to DLQ stream
  - [x] DLQ stream name: `dlq:{original_stream_name}`
  - [x] Store: original message, error info, retry count, timestamp
  - [x] Implement `DLQ.list_failed(stream, limit=100)` for inspection
  - [x] Implement `DLQ.retry(stream, message_id)` to re-enqueue
  - [x] Implement `DLQ.discard(stream, message_id)` to permanently remove
  - [x] Write unit tests (>=85% coverage) — 61 tests, 95% coverage

- [x] 4.8 Add event replay capability
  - [x] Create `elitea_core/utils/event_replay.py`
  - [x] Implement `replay_stream(stream_name, from_id, to_id, target_group)`
  - [x] Uses XRANGE to read historical messages, re-publishes to target
  - [x] Add `--dry-run` mode that counts messages without re-publishing
  - [x] Rate-limit replay to avoid overwhelming consumers (configurable delay)
  - [x] Write unit tests (>=85% coverage) — 44 tests, 96% coverage

- [x] 4.9 Implement event handler timeout
  - [x] Create `elitea_core/utils/handler_timeout.py`
  - [x] Implement `@timeout(seconds=30)` decorator for event handlers
  - [x] On timeout: log warning, NACK the message (returns to pending)
  - [x] Use `signal.alarm` on Linux or threading.Timer as fallback
  - [x] Track timeout occurrences in Redis counter: `metrics:handler_timeouts:{handler_name}`
